          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A claim came back asking for more specificity on a three-character code. Is that always a reasonable request?",
          "answer": "The request for more specificity on a three-character code is not always reasonable. Most ICD-10-CM categories require additional characters beyond the three-character code to be valid. For example, the essential hypertension code (I10) is a complete code at three characters and cannot have additional specificity added. It broadly covers conditions related to high blood pressure without needing further detail. Therefore, asking to add more specificity to I10 would be an invalid request because it does not require further characters for billing (source: \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family\").\n\nIn contrast, many other codes necessitate additional characters to pinpoint specific conditions. It is essential to verify the required length and specificity for each category in the Tabular List, as skipping this step can lead to errors in coding (source: \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\").\n\nWhat I could not cover includes specific examples of situations where additional specificity might be appropriate or how to address claims in practice.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
